OpenBSD PF reference material for 5BSD PF parity
================================================

Source: https://github.com/openbsd/src.git (OpenBSD CVS mirror)
Revision: facc8d7ec5d1f7a22cd5b7f1e8c6ac4990d91244 (2026-10-10), matching the
  OpenBSD 8.0-current amd64 snapshot used as the reference guest; see
  UPSTREAM.json for the snapshot identity, the PF source-alignment check and
  the git tree object ID of every imported subtree.
Imported: 2026-10-11
License: LICENSE (ISC-style OpenBSD notice); per-file notices retained.

Contents (verbatim, upstream relative paths):
  regress/sbin/pfctl, regress/sys/net/pf_{divert,forward,fragment,opts,
  policy,print,state,table,trans}, regress/sys/net/pflog,
  regress/sys/net/pflow                 primary PF parity suites
  regress/sys/netinet/{frag,pmtu,tcpstate}, regress/sys/netinet6/{frag6,rh0}
                                         IP-stack suites using pf as a tool
                                         (secondary, reported separately)
  regress/sys/netinet/ipsec, regress/sys/net/pair
                                         secondary: pflog of IPsec/enc0 traffic
                                         and pflog on pair(4) (epair on 5BSD)

  regress/sys/net/rdomains, regress/sys/net/rtable, regress/sbin/route,
  regress/sbin/ifconfig                 routing domains (rdomain on FIBs) and
                                         route -T / ifconfig rdomain; rtable is
                                         a userland unit test of OpenBSD's ART
                                         routing table code

Not imported (see UPSTREAM.json "not_imported"): usr.sbin/bgpd pftable
integration tests, usr.sbin/relayd (no PF use), sys/kern/pledge/ioctl
(pledge-specific), and suites that only check pf is enabled (sosplice, gif,
loop, mpath, snmpd).
  share/mk/bsd.regress.mk               OpenBSD regress driver; the upstream
                                         Makefiles include it unchanged
  sbin/pfctl                            OpenBSD pfctl (vendor source for the
                                         PF convergence; wired in by
                                         sbin/pfctl/Makefile when adopted)
  usr.sbin/authpf, usr.sbin/ftp-proxy, usr.sbin/tftp-proxy, sbin/pflogd
                                         OpenBSD PF userland (vendor source)
  usr.sbin/tcpdump                      OpenBSD tcpdump (the base tcpdump(8))
  sys/net/{ofp,ethertypes,if_enc}.h, sys/netinet/{ip_ipsp,ip_ether}.h,
  sys/netmpls/mpls.h, sys/dev/usb/usbpcap.h, sys/net80211/ieee80211{,
  _radiotap}.h, usr.sbin/hostapd/iapp.h  headers tcpdump uses (tcpdump only)
  sys/net/pf*.[ch], if_pflog.[ch], if_pflow.[ch], if_pfsync.[ch]
                                         OpenBSD kernel PF (pf.ko and
                                         "device pf")

Not present upstream at this revision: regress for pfsync, queues,
syncookies, authpf, pflogd, ftp-proxy/tftp-proxy.

Local changes: the regress corpus, share/mk and sbin/pfctl are verbatim.
Never edit the regress files to make a test pass; expected outputs change
only through a recorded, user-approved compatibility decision kept in the
glue.  The kernel sources under sys/net carry a small number of edits, each
marked with a "5BSD" comment, where the compatibility layer cannot express
the difference (list: git diff of contrib/openbsd-pf/sys/net against the
import commits; summary below).  Keep them minimal so an update is close
to a re-import.

5BSD edits in sbin/pflogd (marked "5BSD"; glue in sbin/pflogd/pflogd_5bsd.[ch]):
  pflogd.c       libpcap: pflog_read_live() opens pflog(4) through
                 pcap_activate(3) (pflogd_5bsd.c) instead of filling in
                 libpcap's private pcap_t; savefile thiszone is 0.
                 savefile record header: records are written in the 32-bit
                 on-disk layout (struct pcap_sf_pkthdr) because 5BSD's
                 struct pcap_pkthdr carries a struct timeval; the capture
                 length is clamped to the current snaplen because 5BSD's
                 bpf(4) refuses BIOCSETF on a BIOCLOCKed descriptor even
                 for root.
                 pidfile: -p name writes /var/run/<name>.pid (default
                 pflogd.pid), kept from FreeBSD pflogd for rc.d/pflog and
                 newsyslog; openlog() uses LOG_NDELAY so the chrooted child
                 keeps its syslog socket (no sendsyslog(2)).
  privsep.c      libpcap: the privileged process opens pflog before the
                 fork and the child keeps that pcap_t (no re-exec with -P);
                 priv_init_pcap() is then a no-op.
  pflogd.8       -p, the pidfile and the snaplen note above.

5BSD edits in usr.sbin (marked "5BSD"):
  authpf/authpf.c  without fdescfs(4) on /dev/fd, /dev/fd/N above 2 does
                 not exist: pass /dev/pf to pfctl as standard input
                 (/dev/fd/0) instead.
  ftp-proxy/ftp-proxy.c, tftp-proxy/tftp-proxy.c  NOPRIV_USER may be set
                 by the build (5BSD uses the existing "proxy" user)
  ftp-proxy/ftp-proxy.8, tftp-proxy/tftp-proxy.8  a paragraph on the
                 "proxy" user, the 5BSD path (/usr/libexec/tftp-proxy) and
                 the rc.conf(5) knobs

5BSD edits in sys/net (all marked "5BSD"):
  VNET (marked "5BSD: VNET"): global state is defined with PF_VNET_DEFINE*
                 and declared with PF_VNET_DECLARE* (pfvar.h; plain
                 globals outside the kernel), so every vnet runs its own pf;
                 struct tags that share a variable's name are PF_KTAG()ed;
                 static initializers of per-vnet lists move to the glue;
                 pf_norm.c fragment queue/tree are pfn_*; small teardown
                 helpers pfi_vnet_fini(), pf_normalize_vnet_fini(),
                 pf_syncookies_vnet_fini(); tag and queue ids (pf_tags,
                 pf_qids, pf_ioctl.c) are per vnet, initialised in
                 pfattach() and freed by the glue
                 DIOCBINDJAIL ('D' 235, pfvar.h): bind an unused /dev/pf
                 descriptor to a descendant jail's vnet (glue:
                 pf_fbsd_bindjail() in pf_freebsd.c; used by BSDNetwork for
                 system.Network.PF)
  VNET quotas (marked "5BSD: VNET quotas"): DIOCSETLIMIT compares the new
                 limit with pool_nout(), the current vnet's items (pf_ioctl.c);
                 pf_state_expires() passes the default adaptive timeouts
                 through pf_quota_adaptive(), which scales a jail's to its
                 states limit (pf.c).  The pools stay shared, but each vnet
                 is held to its own limit in them (glue:
                 sys/netpfil/openbsd-pf/pf_quota.c and the pool(9) wrappers
                 in openbsd_compat.c); pf_pool_limits[] holds the host's
                 limits, a vnet jail's are the host's scaled by
                 net.pf.vnet_limit_pct and shared with its descendants, and
                 all jails together are capped by net.pf.vnet_total_pct
  m_tag (marked "5BSD: m_tag"): OpenBSD keeps per-packet pf state in
                 m_pkthdr.pf; 5BSD keeps it in an mbuf tag (PACKET_TAG_PF,
                 sys/sys/mbuf.h) so MSIZE stays 256.  Every access is
                 rewritten mechanically:
                   READ   x->m_pkthdr.pf.F   ->  PF_MTAG_R(x)->F
                   WRITE  x->m_pkthdr.pf.F = ->  PF_MTAG_W(x)->F =
                   and pd.m / pd->m / ctx->pd->m  ->  PF_PDTAG_R/W(pd)
                 (writes are =, |=, &=, ++, --).  Reads never allocate
                 (no tag: default values); writes allocate the tag; the
                 PF_PDTAG forms cache it in pf_pdesc (pfh_m, pfh_t).  After
                 a re-import, apply the same rewrite (see the commit
                 "pf: OpenBSD per-packet state in an mbuf tag").  The
                 single exception: PF_TAG_PROCESSED is set only on packets
                 that already have a tag (nothing reads it on 5BSD).
  jail (marked "5BSD: jail"): the 5BSD extension "jail <name|jid>":
                 struct pf_rule_jail and pf_rule.jail (pfvar.h), lookup.jid
                 (pfvar_priv.h), pf_match_jail() and its rule test (pf.c),
                 rule copy and checksum (pf_ioctl.c); pfctl parse.y grammar,
                 pfctl_parser.c printing, pfctl_optimize.c field table
  dummynet (marked "5BSD: dummynet"): the 5BSD extension "dnpipe" and
                 "dnqueue" (dummynet(4) shaping, as in FreeBSD pf):
                 struct pf_rule_dn and pf_rule.dn, pf_rule_actions.dn*
                 (pfvar.h), pf_state.dn* and the glue prototypes
                 (pfvar_priv.h), rule-to-action/state copies, the pf_test()
                 reinjection check and pass-path hand-off and the
                 pf_route()/pf_route6() route-to hand-off (pf.c), rule copy
                 and checksum (pf_ioctl.c); pfctl parse.y grammar,
                 pfctl_parser.c printing, pfctl_optimize.c field table.
                 The logic is in sys/netpfil/openbsd-pf/pf_dummynet.c.
  sctp (marked "5BSD: sctp"): SCTP association tracking, multihoming
                 and the "allow-related" state option, as in FreeBSD pf:
                 the PFTM_SCTP_* timeouts (appended before PFTM_MAX),
                 PFRULE_ALLOW_RELATED, the PF_SCTPS_* peer states and the
                 pfss_v_tag/pfss_ts_mod union in struct pf_state_scrub
                 (pfvar.h); pf_pdesc's SCTP header, chunk flags, initiate
                 tag and related rule, PFDESC_SCTP_*, the glue prototypes
                 (pfvar_priv.h); hooks in pf_setup_pdesc(),
                 pf_create_state(), pf_test_state(), the ICMP-error state
                 lookup, pf_match_rule() ports, pf_test_rule() (related
                 rule, "block return" ABORT, no pfsync deferral for
                 multihome states), pf_state_key_attach() (reuse of closed
                 states), pf_detach_state() and pf_test() (multihome
                 states after filtering) (pf.c); no SCTP port changes for
                 rdr-to (pf_lb.c); default timeouts (pf_ioctl.c); pfctl
                 parse.y grammar (allow-related, SCTP ports and service
                 names), pfctl_parser.c timeout names and printing,
                 pfctl.c default timeouts, pf_print_state.c state names.
                 The logic is in sys/netpfil/openbsd-pf/pf_sctp.c.
                 pfsync: the verification tag travels in pfss_ts_mod, the
                 field it shares with the timestamp modulation, so the
                 OpenBSD wire format is unchanged.  OpenBSD peers do not
                 know the SCTP timeouts and reject SCTP states; the SCTP
                 timeouts take the numbers OpenBSD gives PFTM_PURGE and
                 PFTM_UNLINKED, so such a timeout from an OpenBSD peer is
                 read as sctp.opening or sctp.established.
  ether (marked "5BSD: ether"): the 5BSD extension "ether" rules (layer-2
                 filtering, as in FreeBSD pf): struct pf_eth_rule,
                 struct pfioc_eth_rule, PF_TRANS_ETH and the DIOC*ETHRULE*
                 ioctls ('D' 230-234) (pfvar.h), the glue prototypes
                 (pfvar_priv.h), PF_TRANS_ETH elements in DIOCXBEGIN,
                 DIOCXROLLBACK and DIOCXCOMMIT (pf_ioctl.c).  The rulesets,
                 ioctls, packet path and pfil(9) hooks are in
                 sys/netpfil/openbsd-pf/pf_ether.c.  pfctl: parse.y
                 grammar and keywords (ether, l3, bridge-to,
                 ridentifier), struct pfctl.eth and the prototypes
                 (pfctl_parser.h), loading in pfctl_rules(), -s ethernet,
                 -s Anchors, -s all, -z, -F ethernet and -F all (pfctl.c);
                 the rest is in sys/netpfil/openbsd-pf/user/pfctl_ether.c.
  pfvar.h        REASON_SET fires the pf::reason:set DTrace probe
                 userland (!_KERNEL): sys/param.h before sys/syslimits.h;
                 PF_DIVERT #undef, RTLABEL_LEN and union sockaddr_union
                 (from other OpenBSD headers) so it is the installed
                 <net/pfvar.h>
  pfvar_priv.h   state-list lock is a sleepable lock (struct sx)
  pf.c           receive interface from m_pkthdr.rcvif (no ph_ifidx);
                 flow id through M_HASHTYPE; DTrace probe hooks;
                 pf_socket_lookup() uses the outbound packet's socket
                 (locked by the 5BSD stack) instead of a pcb table lookup
  pf_if.c        CK_STAILQ interface/group lists; allocate before PF_LOCK;
                 "5BSD: names" (interfaces sharing a name share a kif;
                 pfi_kif_match_ifp() and pf_pdesc.ifp match groups and
                 flags on the packet's own interface; pf_routable_ifp()
                 and PF_MISMATCHAW_IFP() (pf.c, pfvar.h) judge urpf-failed
                 by it too)
  pf_ioctl.c     p_pid; pfioctl_rw is sleepable; overload table PR_NOWAIT
                 under PF_LOCK; DTrace probe hooks; DIOCADDSTATE rejects
                 (EINVAL) the states pfsync input rejects: timeout, peer
                 states, direction, address families (OpenBSD: timeout)
                 pf_hash_rule() adds the 5BSD jail and dummynet fields
                 only for rules that use them, so other rulesets have
                 OpenBSD's checksum (pfsync rule matching with OpenBSD)
  pf_norm.c      refragmented packets leave through if_output_obsd()
  pf_lb.c, pf_table.c, pf_syncookies.c   DTrace probe hooks
  if_pflog.[ch], if_pflow.[ch], if_pfsync.c
                 ifnet is if_alloc()ed (sc_if is a macro over sc_ifp);
                 if_output casts; interface counters; no ifq start;
                 sleepable configuration locks; DTrace probe hooks;
                 pflow_clone_destroy() drains its timeouts and waits out
                 PF_LOCK holders (export_pflow() outside the net epoch)
  carp (marked "5BSD: carp"): pfsync's carp(4) demotion is compiled in
                 (if_pfsync.c defines PFSYNC_CARP_DEMOTE before "carp.h";
                 pf.c's IFT_CARP paths stay out and pfsync0 joins no "carp"
                 group, carp(4) not being an interface).  The carp group
                 demote counts map onto net.inet.carp.demotion through
                 carp_group_demote_adj() and net.pfsync.carp_demotion_factor
                 (pf_pfsync.c).  The syncdev link-state hook runs under the
                 net lock and only while pfsync is running
                 (pfsync_syncif_link_task), as OpenBSD's synchronous hooks
                 do, so the link demotion stays balanced.
  pfsync6 (marked "5BSD: pfsync6"): the 5BSD extension of an IPv6 sync
                 peer: struct pfsyncreq6 and pfsync_input6() (if_pfsync.h);
                 SIOC[GS]ETPFSYNC6 in pfsync_[gs]et_ioc(), the peer in the
                 softc (struct pfsync6, openbsd_compat.h), hooks in
                 pfsync_up/down/set_mtu/sendout, and ipackets/ipackets6
                 counted in pfsync_input4/6 (if_pfsync.c).  The ioctl
                 numbers are in sys/sys/sockio.h, the logic in pf_pfsync.c,
                 the IPv6 input registration in pf_freebsd.c.  pfsync
                 messages are unchanged: the IPv4 header pfsync builds is
                 replaced by an IPv6 one (hop limit 255), and the pfsync
                 MTU stays 20 bytes below the syncdev's.  SIOC[GS]ETPFSYNC
                 keep OpenBSD's struct and leave the IPv6 peer alone.
                 A bulk request timeout that arrives after a state change
                 (the callout ran as pfsync_down() deleted it) is dropped,
                 not an invalid event: sc_bulk_req.req_tmo_at.
The compatibility layer and 5BSD attachment live in sys/netpfil/openbsd-pf
(openbsd_compat.[ch], openbsd_net.c, openbsd_ifq.c, pf_freebsd.c,
pf_pfsync.c, pf_sdt.c, include/ shadow headers, user/ userland
compatibility).

5BSD glue: tests/sys/netpfil/pf/openbsd (Kyua/ATF wrapper; installs this
corpus beneath its srctree/ through tests/srctree.mk) and
tools/test/pf-parity/openbsd-regress (two-guest topology runner: OpenBSD
helper as SRC/RT/ECO, DUT = OpenBSD reference or 5BSD).

OpenBSD-derived PF code: whole files adopted for parity are imported here
with provenance and wired in from their native build directories.  Code
merged into 5BSD files (ifconfig, headers) is recorded below (OpenBSD file,
revision, local destination) and in the commit message.  FreeBSD's
sys/netpfil/pf, libpfctl and pf netlink families are retired
(sys/netpfil/pf keeps only pf.h, pf_altq.h and pf_mtag.h, which the
kernel ALTQ disciplines still include).

Adopted OpenBSD code (pf/parity), all from facc8d7ec5d1:
  sys/net pf*.c, if_pflog, if_pflow, if_pfsync, hfsc, fq_codel, radix (for
  pf tables, renamed obsd_rn_*), sys/netinet in4_cksum.c, inet_nat64.c
                         -> pf.ko (sys/modules/pf)
  sbin/pfctl, etc/pf.os  -> /sbin/pfctl, /etc/pf.os
  share/man/man4 pf.4, pflog.4, pflow.4, pfsync.4, divert.4, share/man/man5
  pf.conf.5, pf.os.5     -> installed by share/man/man4 and man5 (FreeBSD's
                            ipdivert.4 is retired); marked 5BSD
                            additions: pf.conf.5 jail, dummynet, ether rules and
                            queueing status, pfctl.8 ethernet and rc.conf(5),
                            pf.4 VNET and DIOCBINDJAIL, divert.4 (the only
                            divert interface), pfsync.4 IPv6 peers and carp
                            demotion, pf.4/pflog.4/pfsync.4/pflow.4
                            "device pf" for OpenBSD's pseudo-device lines
  usr.sbin/authpf, ftp-proxy, tftp-proxy -> same programs
  usr.sbin/tcpdump      -> /usr/sbin/tcpdump (glue: usr.sbin/tcpdump/tcpdump:
                            openbsd_compat.h, include/, privsep_5bsd.c)
  sys/net/pfvar.h, if_pflog.h, if_pfsync.h, if_pflow.h, hfsc.h, fq_codel.h
                         -> /usr/include/net (include/Makefile); with
                            sys/netpfil/openbsd-pf/user/sys/{refcnt,timeout}.h
                            -> /usr/include/sys
  sbin/ifconfig/ifconfig.c pfsync/pflow functions -> sbin/ifconfig/ifpf.c
  sys/sys/mbuf.h struct pkthdr_pf and PF_TAG_* -> 5BSD sys/sys/mbuf.h
  sys/sys/sockio.h SIOC[SG]IFPARENT, SIOC[GS]ETPF{SYNC,LOW} numbers and
  sys/net/if.h struct if_parent -> 5BSD headers

Update procedure: fetch the new pinned commit, replace the subtrees,
regenerate UPSTREAM.json (git rev-parse <commit>:<path>), rerun the reference
baseline on the matching signed OpenBSD snapshot, and record any expected
output changes in the PF parity gap report.

Regression plumbing adjustment (5BSD):
  regress/sys/net/pflow/Makefile escapes Perl $@ as $$@ so bmake does not
  replace it with its automatic target variable. Test expectations unchanged.
  regress/sys/net/pflog/Makefile uses portable UID shell assignment and
  sysctl -n values so bmake checks the real UID and forwarding settings;
  packet-output expectations and forwarding requirements are unchanged.
  regress/sys/net/rdomains/ifalocal.sh calls its defined fail_test helper
  so a failed route-count assertion cannot report success. Expectations unchanged.
